%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: ad50d8bdf54d4c47a3aa5348e08dc8b0, type: 3}
  m_Name: GameGenre
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Multiplayer.Center.Editor::Unity.Multiplayer.Center.Window.GameGenreDescription
  Descriptions:
  - Genre: 0
    Name: Casual co-op
    Description: Casual co-op games center friendly teamwork and communication. Common
      game types are collaborative puzzle games, group exploration, and co-op platformers.
    Header: {fileID: 9197481963319205126, guid: a73e3729fc91e544c931c37ebe21ea93, type: 3}
    Complexity: {x: 36, y: 68}
    ComplexityDescription: Casual co-op games don't require the extra infrastructure 
      needed for competitive games. Players act as hosts, removing the need for a 
      dedicated server, and there are fewer concerns around matchmaking and management 
      because players are often playing with friends.
    ComplexitySummary: Medium
    Cost: {x: 0, y: 56}
    CostDescription: In a casual co-op game one of the players hosts the game  session
      on their own machine. While live, the game coordinates  network information
      which incurs a small cost for the game developer. The main technical costs
      of hosting (game simulation) are on the player themselves."
    CostSummary: Low/Medium
    Architecture: Casual co-op games can use an authoritative server or a peer-to-peer
      client server for more casual games. The game needs to create rooms (or sessions)
      for players to interact in and as well as a system for synchronizing the game
      state across instances.
    ArchitectureSummary: Client hosting
  - Genre: 1
    Name: Competitive
    Description: In fast-paced competitive games, multiplayer gameplay involves 
      constantly exchanging player actions and game state information in real  time.
      Common games types are first-person shooters, fighting games,  and battle royales.
    Header: {fileID: 9197481963319205126, guid: ff431a7b3eb8b134d94706de7fbcbd93, type: 3}
    Complexity: {x: 46, y: 100}
    ComplexityDescription: 'Competitive games require more effort to set up and manage
      over time compared to casual and turn-based games. Hosting requirements, server
      locations, and many other pieces need to be considered. '
    ComplexitySummary: Medium/High
    Cost: {x: 46, y: 100}
    CostDescription: Competitive games require an arbiter to ensure no cheating is
      taking place, so an authoritative game simulation is  run on a dedicated server.
      This simulation requires more resources which leads to a higher cost.
    CostSummary: Medium/High
    Architecture: Competitive games most often use a client-server model with an
      authoritative server that enforces the rules of the game to enforce fairness
      across the players. These games are often fast-paced and require netcode optimization
      for the best user experiences, or additional tools like client-side prediction
      systems to create smoother experiences and deal with latency between users.
    ArchitectureSummary: Dedicated servers
  - Genre: 2
    Name: Turn-based
    Description: "In turn-based games, players don't share all information in
      real time and take turns to influence the game state. Common game types are
      strategy games, board games, and card games."
    Header: {fileID: 9197481963319205126, guid: 3965d8f06d27b5e48b5ca2725c7c4adc, type: 3}
    Complexity: {x: 0, y: 34}
    ComplexityDescription: Turn-based games have fewer requirements than competitive
      real-time games but still require an authoritative server, state management
      and functionality around player management. Games need to communicate a shared
      game state consistently and enforce game rules.
    ComplexitySummary: Low
    Cost: {x: 0, y: 12}
    CostDescription: Event-driven games require some functions  that run on a server
      or database, which adds to the overall cost. However, because these functions
      run intermittently, they cost less than continuously simulating a game on a
      server.
    CostSummary: Low
    Architecture: Turn-based games are built around a shared game state that changes
      through clearly ordered player actions which are validated by a server. Players
      take turns making decisions, and the game records, validates, and applies those
      decisions in sequence so that everyone sees the same board, battle, or strategic
      situation. The architecture focuses less on fast movement and more on reliable
      state progression, turn order, and rule-driven outcomes.
    ArchitectureSummary: Event Driven
